**API interpolation:**
State: `prev` = {pos3, lat, lon, t_ms}, `target` = same. On new API sample: prev = current interpolated position (or prev target), target = new sample's position. Progress p = clamp01((now - sampleTime)/POLL_MS). Slerp: `pos = prevPos.clone().slerp?` Three's Vector3 has no slerp... actually Vector3 has no slerp, but you can do it manually: normalize both, angle = acos(clamp(dot)), sinA, result = (prev*sin((1-p)A) + target*sin(p*A))/sinA. Handle small angle.

Simpler: use quaternion slerp: `new Quaternion().setFromUnitVectors(prevNorm, targetNorm)` and `q = prevQuat.slerp?` Vector3 slerp is easy:

```js
function slerpVec(a, b, t){
  const dot = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(a.dot(b), -1, 1);
  const theta = Math.acos(dot);
  if (theta < 1e-6) return a.clone().lerp(b, t);
  const st = Math.sin(theta);
  const wa = Math.sin((1-t)*theta)/st;
  const wb = Math.sin(t*theta)/st;
  return a.clone().multiplyScalar(wa).add(b.clone().multiplyScalar(wb));
}
```

Ground speed: great-circle distance from prev sample's lat/lon to new sample's lat/lon (in km), divided by (t_new - t_old)/1000 hours... `km / (dt/3600000)` = km/h.

**API timestamp**: seconds. `sampleTime = ts*1000`. Note: API timestamp is the time of the ephemeris computation, which may be slightly in the past/present. Interpolation window: progress = (now - ts*1000)/POLL_MS clamped to [0,1].

