```js
function fixPos(fix, out) {
  const r = EARTH_R * (EARTH_R_KM + fix.alt) / EARTH_R_KM;
  return ecef(fix.lat, fix.lon, r, out);
}
function onFix(fix) {
  if (prevFix) {
    const dt = (fix.tMs - prevFix.tMs) / 1000;
    if (dt > 0) {
      const p1 = fixPos(prevFix, tmpA);
      const p2 = fixPos(fix, tmpB);
      const a1 = latLonFromVec(p1), a2 = latLonFromVec(p2);
      const dKm = haversine(a1[0], a1[1], a2[0], a2[1]);
      groundSpeed = dKm / dt;
    }
  }
  prevFix = nextFix;
  nextFix = fix;
  lastFixMs = performance.now();
  if (!firstFixDone) { firstFixDone = true; stepDone('FIRST FIX'); }
  if (lastApiCrossCheckDone === false) doSolarCrossCheck(fix);
}
```

Wait: interpolation logic — we want to slerp between prevFix and nextFix with progress based on wall clock. When a new fix arrives, the previous "nextFix" becomes "prevFix". p = clamp01((nowMs - prevFix.tMs)/(nextFix.tMs - prevFix.tMs)). If p >= 1 (waited between polls), stay at nextFix position (and the next fix extends it). In practice, fixes are about 2-5 seconds old (API timestamp is generation time), and we poll every 5 seconds, so p advances 0→~1 over about 5 seconds. Good.

